ESL positions overseas in mind. Feel free to implement what you like and di=
sregard what you don&#39;t. It&#39;s of course ultimately your document. <b=
r><br>That said, you have hired me to do this review and give you unbiased =
and direct feedback, so I will not hold back in any area I think you can im=
prove. =C2=A0<br><br>When I first open a resume, I like 5 things to jump ou=
t at me immediately before scrolling down. These are: <br><br>1.) Native le=
vel speaker <br>2.) Degrees<br>3.) Certs <br>4.) Skype<br>5.) Availability =
<br><br>These are the five pieces of data that most everyone looking throug=
h resumes is seeking.=C2=A0 According to Statistic Brain, the human attenti=
on span is 8 seconds, which is 1 second shorter than a goldfish, so it&#39;=
s especially important that this information be easy to read.=C2=A0
<div><br></div>
<div><br><br>Starting from the top, I would: <br><br>1.) put USA after WV<b=
r><br>2.) put a +1 in front of my phone number <br><br>3.) consider listing=
 a mailing address <br><br><br><br>These three items above lend credibility=
 and remind the reader subtly that you have a home in the US and you are an=
 American and native speaker. <br><br><br>I am a big proponent of creating =
summary statements that immediately grab the attention of the reader. I lik=
e to let them know within 2 seconds of opening our document why they need t=
o give us the interview. Instead of telling the reader what we&#39;re looki=
ng for, I like to show what we have to offer. I&#39;d consider creating a b=
old statement between your contact details and education sections that show=
s what you bring to the table. Based on what I&#39;m reading here, it might=
 be something like:=C2=A0</div>
<div><br></div>
<div><br></div>
<div><strong><br>TEFL-Certified Professional Educator with Bachelor&#39;s D=
egree and American Passport Available Immediately=C2=A0</strong></div>
<div><br></div>
<div><br></div>
<div><br></div>
<div><br>^^If I were looking for a qualified teacher and read this upon ope=
ning your resume, you&#39;d absolutely have my attention. You show right of=
f the bat that you check all the boxes of the average employer abroad. <br>=
<br><br>Education section: <br><br>I&#39;d list USA after WV. Many people a=
re unfamiliar with US cities and states. It&#39;s totally up to you if you =
want to list graduation dates, but I would not here. It will bring attentio=
n to the fact that you&#39;re a recent graduate. I wouldn&#39;t deny it, bu=
t I just wouldn&#39;t highlight it. I would drop the GPA as well. <br><br><=
br><br>Professional Experience: <br><br>I would put USA after all of these =
city and state combos.=C2=A0</div>
<div><br></div>
<div><br><br>My honest feeling is that we should seriously consider droppin=
g the jobs that have really short durations. I have been beaten up in inter=
views so many times because of 3-6 month jobs that I have deleted them from=
 my resume over the years. Why? <br><br>Employers are trained to look for r=
ed flags just like we look for red flags with them. They look for a few thi=
ngs in ESL: <br><br>1.) Very short duration employment. This could potentia=
lly show you&#39;re a flight risk. Many people list every short term positi=
on they&#39;ve ever had and employers say &quot;They are going to leave aft=
er 3 months. Too risky. We don&#39;t want to invest in the visa and getting=
 him over here if there&#39;s a high likelihood he won&#39;t last.&quot; Th=
ey often gloss over these too... and I&#39;d hate for them to dismiss your =
resume without realizing what were you doing at all these jobs back to back=
.=C2=A0</div>
<div><br><br>2.) Listing a lot of intern and tutoring jobs. Many people pad=
 their resumes with this because it&#39;s often unverifiable experience. I =
have dropped this from my resume because I got tired of explaining myself--=
just like with short duration employment. I&#39;m sure countless others scr=
apped my resume without allowing me to explain.=C2=A0<br><br><br>3.) Freque=
ntly changing locations. This brings about concern for obvious reasons. <br=
><br><br><br>Your Evoqua experience is solid. It&#39;s a year and a half in=
 duration and it&#39;s obvious you did a lot. If you had any experience tra=
ining, instructing, working with people whose native language was not Engli=
sh, etc... I would go into that in more detail. <br><br><br><br>The one tim=
e I would seriously consider keeping this intern experience in here is if I=
 were applying to specific Safety English programs like with Saudi Aramco o=
r something similar. In that case, it could be useful to show you&#39;ve ha=
d the training. <br><br><br>Research Experience: <br><br>This looks great. =
<br><br>Skills: <br><br>It&#39;s up to you if you want to leave this on her=
e or not. If you&#39;re planning to teach computer stuff, this could be use=
ful. For ESL, I think honestly that most people won&#39;t know these progra=
ms and acronyms. <br><br><br><br>Your activities look great. The only thing=
 to point out is that under Mountaineer United, you begin to use punctuatio=
n in your final two bullets. I would delete it so that these match the form=
at of the rest of your resume. <br><br><br>Next step:=C2=A0</div>
<div><br>I think with a few changes implemented, you&#39;ll be happy with h=
ow this turns out. I&#39;ve attached some design options to this email to h=
elp conceptualize how the drafts will look. I have your photo (which is gre=
